  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A round of showers and thunderstorms this\nevening into the overnight period. \n\nAnother short wave moving eastward across the Appalachians this\nevening will continue to progress through MD/VA tonight. It \nwill create the opportunity for more isolated to scattered \nshowers and tstms across (mostly) southern and western parts of \nthe CWA. We have preserved the 30%-40% pops for the evening and \nearly overnight periods. While only the SW corner of our \nDelmarva areas are in Day 1 SPC outlook, strong winds gusts are \npossible with any tstm. The activity diminishes after \nmidnight-2AM and skies will become partly cloudy following it. \nPatchy fog across rural areas that receive any rain. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mainly dry and comfortable weather expected for \nLabor Day Weekend.\n\nBy Saturday, the trend among model guidance shows the frontal \nboundary south of our region, with Canadian high pressure \nbuilding in across the Northeast and Mid-Atlantic. This will \nlargely end any shower and thunderstorm potential, however a\nstray shower or two cannot entirely be ruled out. All in all, \nLabor Day Weekend is still shaping up to feature nice weather, \nwith temperatures trending at or slightly below normal and\ncomfortable humidity levels.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Minor tidal flooding is anticipated with Sunday\nafternoon's high tide, mainly for the Jersey Shore, Delaware \nBeaches, and Delaware Bay communities.\n\nFlow will turn more onshore over the next few days. This will\nresult in water piling up over consecutive tide cycles,\npotentially leading to minor tidal flooding across portions of\nour area on Sunday afternoon. The current forecast has minor \ntidal flooding from Barnegat Bay on south along the Atlantic \nOcean coastline and for areas within Delaware Bay. Advisories \nmay be needed in these spots but will wait to see how things \ntrend over the 12 to 24 hours with the forecast.\n\nWithin the tidal Delaware River, northern NJ coast, and\nChesapeake Bay, tidal flooding will be limited to spotty minor\nor no flooding at all. Tide levels should decrease getting into\nnext week as flow turns more offshore. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 4...A cold front will pass through on Thursday \nbringing the potential for another round of showers and storms.\n\nBy Tuesday, high pressure will begin to shift offshore welcoming\nthe return of slightly above-normal temperatures and an increase\nin humidity levels. High pressure looks like it will still be \nin control through mid-week, before giving way to a cold front\nthat will be approaching from the north and west Wednesday night\ninto Thursday. This front will be the next best opportunity for\nany showers or thunderstorms around the area. As of now, PoPs\nare in the 20-30% range.",
